# LeakSanitizer suppressions for the rawform UI app.
#
# The PROVEN floor of leak suppressions is baked into the sanitizer builds
# (utils/SanitizerSuppressions.cpp, __lsan_default_suppressions), because
# env-var delivery through IDE run configurations proved fragile. This file
# is the no-rebuild STAGING overlay for triage, added ON TOP of the baked
# list when pointed at through the environment of the RUN (not the build):
#
#   LSAN_OPTIONS=suppressions=/path/to/rawform/ui/tools/lsan.supp
#
# An entry that proves out here graduates into SanitizerSuppressions.cpp.
#
# ADMISSION CRITERIA, strict by design (same bar as tsan.supp): a pattern
# lands here only when every frame of the reported allocation stack sits in
# third-party code (Qt platform plugins, system libraries) AND the leak is a
# process-lifetime singleton the library never frees on exit, so it cannot
# implicate rawform code and cannot grow over a session. A leak that recurs
# per action (per file, per track, per dialog open) does NOT qualify, however
# third-party its stack looks: growth means a real bug somewhere.
#
# ONE STATED EXCEPTION: a per-event leak whose every allocation frame is
# third-party AND whose cause is a located upstream defect (the frames name
# the function, the library's source shows the re-entry or the missing free)
# qualifies when the event is bounded by something the user does a handful
# of times per session (a window destroyed, a thread exited), the cost per
# event is fixed and small, and no rawform change short of replacing an
# asset or a library would remove it. The entry names the defect, the
# trigger and the cost, so it is re-examined when the library moves.

# libxkbcommon keymap/compose state, allocated once by the Qt Wayland platform
# plugin (QWaylandDisplay::setupConnection and the text-input compose table
# the first time a text field takes a key). Held for the life of the process
# and not released by the Wayland client teardown path; reported at exit.
# Typing in any text field "triggers" the compose-table half, which is why it
# first surfaced against the rename dialog's pattern field; the field is
# incidental. Graduated into SanitizerSuppressions.cpp; kept here so a run
# with a stale binary still has it.
leak:libxkbcommon

# Qt's per-thread FreeType data against variable fonts (qfontengine_ft.cpp,
# Qt 6.11.1): when a QThread that rasterized text exits, QThreadStorage
# nulls the thread's QtFreetypeData slot and then runs its destructor, which
# calls QFreetypeFace::cleanup() on every cached face; for a face with
# variation data (mm_var, set only for a variable font) cleanup() calls
# FT_Done_MM_Var(qt_getFreetype(), mm_var), and qt_getFreetypeData() finds
# the slot empty and allocates a fresh QtFreetypeData plus a fresh FreeType
# library to serve the call. QThreadStorageData::finish then nulls the
# re-created slot without deleting it (its own comment says so). 48 bytes
# direct plus 2448 bytes of FreeType modules indirect, 2496 in 33
# allocations, once per exiting thread that drew text: in rawform the scene
# graph render thread of a window that is destroyed (a Properties window
# closing), not the hidden-and-reused windows. Triggered by the bundled
# Inter and JetBrains Mono variable fonts; a static font takes the other
# branch. Every frame is libQt6Gui, libQt6Core or libfreetype; rawform
# appears nowhere. Anchored on the defect's own frame, present in all nine
# stacks. Re-examine on every Qt bump: a fixed Qt makes the entry dead.
leak:QFreetypeFace::cleanup
